+ {"id":"CF-074","title":"20 of 22 complete rows have no closure-evidence entry a stranger can resolve — the doctrine has no gate, so each closure mints a fresh unresolvable digest","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the review lane (tick code-factory-review-2026-11-03-14-58-22) in a throwaway clone of origin/main at 64c0c10828525e091a3a8747a38fd8d2e299a81e, board read at 58 rows / 22 complete.\n\nThe repository has a written closure doctrine (docs/traceability-doctrine.md, rules 2/4/6: evidence is a produced artifact, a closing verdict binds to a PUBLISHED commit, and every exact repository-relative artifact must resolve in the published tree; plus the board's own CF-047 reading that a 40/64-hex value which `git cat-file -t` does not resolve is not evidence). Nothing enforces it. tests/selftest.sh section 7 parses the JSONL and stops there; `grep -rln 'ch:trace' scripts tests` matches only selftest.sh, and no script validates detail.evidence. The result is a per-closure drift that today covers almost every closed row:\n\nA mechanical audit of every COMPLETE row's detail.evidence (script and full transcript reproduced in the lane evidence file, /home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/review/code-factory/review-2026-11-03-evidence.md) finds only TWO complete rows with an evidence entry a stranger can resolve in a fresh clone: CF-034 and CF-055. The other 20 of 22 are unresolvable, and not for exotic reasons:\n\n1. One single fingerprint, 59f476975880e46d49eec1fbaec75b289ced048f4e669855d2cee386061fb5ec, is pasted across EIGHT closures (CF-009, CF-010, CF-014, CF-015, CF-030, CF-032, CF-033, CF-035). `git cat-file -t` on it prints \"fatal: Not a valid object name\"; no document anywhere in the tree states the algorithm that would regenerate it (`grep -rn tree_fp --include='*.md'` returns no definition). Eight closures therefore rest on one value that resolves to nothing and cannot be recomputed.\n2. Five more complete rows carry a