ny COMPLETE row has no evidence entry whose artifacts resolve in `git ls-tree -r <ref>` AND whose hex tree_fp resolves via `git cat-file -t` to a git object and is not all zeros; it exits 0 when every complete row has one such entry. Re-run today at origin/main it must exit 1 naming at least the 20 row ids in this row's reasoning (CF-009,010,011,012,013,014,015,030,032,033,035,036,037,038,039,041,052,054,058,PERF-012); after the amendments it must exit 0. Paste both invocations and both exit codes into the evidence artifact.\n\n2. The audit is wired into tests/selftest.sh as its own numbered section and the wiring is proven causal: with the pre-fix board (origin/main at b430bed, or a copy of it) the section prints FAIL naming >=20 row ids and the run exits nonzero; with the amended board it prints PASS. Record both runs, including the section's own output lines, in the evidence artifact.\n\n3. The shared fingerprint 59f476975880e46d49eec1fbaec75b289ced048f4e669855d2cee386061fb5ec is retired the documented way for all eight rows that carry it (CF-009, CF-010, CF-014, CF-015, CF-030, CF-032, CF-033, CF-035): each row either gains an appended evidence entry whose fingerprint `git cat-file -t <fp>` resolves to a tree, or records `none:<reason>` PLUS the exact command (algorithm) that regenerates the value at HEAD and the value it regenerates to. A bare re-paste of the same unresolvable digest does not satisfy this criterion.\n\n4. CF-032's second evidence entry stops resting on an unpublished ref: its run_id either names a commit that `git cat-file -t` resolves in a FRESH clone of origin (not only via refs/gitreins/history in the owner's checkout) or is superseded by an appended entry with `none:<reason>`, and its empty artifacts[] is replaced by at least one repository-relative artifact that `git cat-file -e <ref>:<path>` resolves.\n\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the closing commit, and `jq -c . .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` parses every lin